      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Base rate/reference class: for the same WIC total-participation variant, the three fetched May observations were 6.696739 million in 2023, 6.763710 million in 2024, and 6.894610 million in 2025. The three historical November-to-May moves were +0.300006 million from Nov 2022 to May 2023, +0.139673 million from Nov 2023 to May 2024, and +0.065289 million from Nov 2024 to May 2025."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level and momentum: the clean May-to-May trend points upward, with May 2025 above May 2024 by 0.130900 million and May 2024 above May 2023 by 0.066971 million. The latest initial November 2025 level is unusually low relative to October 2025, so I discount a pure Nov 2025 persistence forecast and treat part of the drop as reporting/timing or temporary churn risk rather than a permanent level shift."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Policy and mechanism: WIC participation is driven by categorical eligibility, income eligibility, state administration, births/young-child caseload, and take-up. I do not see a May 2026 core-program policy change in the fetched official tables; proposed FY2027 benefit changes would begin after this May target, so they are not a direct level cut for this observation."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence prior uses the historical Nov-to-May reference class from fetched FNS monthly totals: changes were 0.300006, 0.139673, and 0.065289 million, average = 0.168323, so Nov 2025 initial 6.752138 + 0.168323 = 6.920461 million. May-trend prior uses May 2025 plus average of the two May-to-May gains: 6.894610 + ((0.066971 + 0.130900)/2) = 6.993546 million. I weight the persistence prior about 45% and the May-trend prior about 55%, then apply a small caution for the weak Nov 2025 first print, giving 6.960 million. Horizon-matched dispersion from the three Nov-to-May changes gives sigma = 0.119953 million; 1.28*sigma = 0.1535 million, rounded to 0.154. The three-observation volatility sample is thin, so this 80% interval is approximate, but it remains tied to the realized same-series dispersion: 6.960 - 0.154 = 6.806 and 6.960 + 0.154 = 7.114 million."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Counter-considerations: upside risk is a rebound from the depressed November 2025 initial count plus stronger child caseload growth, which would land above the interval if May first print exceeds 7.114 million. Downside risk is a persistent administrative or funding disruption carrying through spring 2026, or a genuine caseload drop rather than a temporary November print, which would land below the interval if May first print is under 6.806 million. Outside the interval would therefore require either a larger-than-historical spring rebound or a continuation of the November 2025 shock."
      },
